    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_plan_011",
      "dimension_source": "rubric",
      "dimension_name": "Sequencing logic",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 2,
      "comment": "The chronological order from 1870 to 1880 and then 1900 is sensible for tracking Patrick as an adult. The 1880 census is appropriately prioritized for its relationship-to-head information. However, the plan does not place FamilySearch searches before corresponding Ancestry searches because no Ancestry items or fallback chains were created. The earlier marriage record could also have been placed before the census searches to help identify Thomas's wife."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_plan_011",
      "dimension_source": "base",
      "dimension_name": "Correctness",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 2,
      "comment": "I checked the project facts and the plan items. The skill correctly identified the completed earlier plan, the evidence from the 1850 and 1860 censuses, and the death certificate naming Thomas Flynn as Patrick's father. It also correctly recognized the value of the 1880 relationship column and Patrick's 1908 death date. However, it missed the required Ancestry fallback item linked to a corresponding FamilySearch census item through fallback_for."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_plan_011",
      "dimension_source": "base",
      "dimension_name": "Completeness",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 2,
      "comment": "The plan covers all three requested census years and includes relevant searches for Thomas Flynn and James Brown, as well as the marriage record. It correctly avoids repeating the completed 1850 and 1860 searches. However, it omits all Ancestry items and the required fallback_for chain. This leaves an explicit requirement of the research plan incomplete."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_research_plan_rws",
      "dimension_source": "rubric",
      "dimension_name": "Sequencing logic",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 2,
      "comment": "I checked the order of the nine items and their dependencies. The sequence is generally sensible because it starts with searchable records, then moves to original parish records and harder-to-access probate records. However, item 5 is described as a fallback for item 1, but its fallback_for field is null. The plan should formally link the parish-register search to the indexed baptism search so the fallback relationship is preserved. Also, the county birth register could be placed earlier because it is the key civil record for Margaret's 1897 birth."
    },
    {
